  <section id="uce-brief" class="uce-section"><div class="uce-wrap uce-two-col"><div><p class="uce-eyebrow">01 / Give the campaign a clear job</p><h2>Start with the decision<br>your buyer needs to make.</h2><p class="uce-body-lead">A US B2B lead-generation campaign connects a defined offer with the people who may need it. Cold calling opens the conversation; qualification establishes whether a sales meeting has a useful purpose.</p><p>For this example, the software supports invoice approval alongside an existing finance system. The first conversation explores how approvals work across locations and whether the controller wants to examine a different workflow.</p><p>The caller has a clear question, and the salesperson knows what to explore.</p></div><div class="uce-brief-table"><div><span>Campaign decision</span><span>Our starting brief</span></div><dl><div><dt>Account fit</dt><dd>Industrial distributors with several US locations and finance oversight across those sites.</dd></div><div><dt>Buyer role</dt><dd>Controller or finance operations leader responsible for invoice processing and approval.</dd></div><div><dt>Problem to explore</dt><dd>Whether approvals depend on email, repeated chasing or unclear ownership. Confirm it in conversation.</dd></div><div><dt>Starting territory</dt><dd>Selected Eastern and Central time zone accounts. Schedule against the buyer’s actual location.</dd></div><div><dt>Meeting purpose</dt><dd>Review the approval workflow and see whether the software merits a technical evaluation.</dd></div></dl></div></div></section>

  <section id="uce-accounts" class="uce-section uce-paper"><div class="uce-wrap"><div class="uce-section-heading"><div><p class="uce-eyebrow">02 / Account selection</p><h2>A useful list includes<br>reasons to leave people out.</h2></div><p>Both sample companies are distributors. Only one fits the campaign’s starting assumptions.</p></div><div class="uce-account-grid"><article class="uce-account"><p class="uce-label">Sample account A / Research next</p><h3>A distributor with three locations.</h3><p>The company profile shows several US locations and an identified controller. That supports further research and a relevant first call.</p><dl><div><dt>What we know</dt><dd>Industrial distribution, three locations, a finance owner.</dd></div><div><dt>What we still need to learn</dt><dd>How invoices are approved, whether anything is difficult and who would evaluate a change.</dd></div></dl><p class="uce-verdict"><strong>Decision:</strong> include for research and outreach. Multiple locations alone do not prove a software need.</p></article><article class="uce-account"><p class="uce-label">Sample account B / Exclude from this brief</p><h3>A single-location trade counter.</h3><p>The available profile shows one location and a different operating setup. It falls outside this campaign’s agreed multi-location focus.</p><dl><div><dt>What we know</dt><dd>One location; the defined account criteria are not met.</dd></div><div><dt>What we should avoid assuming</dt><dd>That every distributor has complex approvals or that every finance contact needs a demo.</dd></div></dl><p class="uce-verdict"><strong>Decision:</strong> leave it out of this campaign. Revisit only if the targeting brief changes.</p></article></div></div></section>

  <section id="uce-conversation" class="uce-section"><div class="uce-wrap"><div class="uce-section-heading"><div><p class="uce-eyebrow">03 / The cold call</p><h2>Open the subject.<br>Let the buyer shape the next step.</h2></div><p>A relevant question gives the controller room to describe the work. The caller then follows what the buyer actually says.</p></div><div class="uce-opening"><p class="uce-label">Illustrative opening / Accounts-payable software</p><blockquote>“Hi [First name], it’s [Your name] with [Company]. We help finance teams manage invoice approvals across locations. How do approvals work between your branches and the finance team today?”</blockquote><p>The opening names the workflow and asks about the current process. It does not claim to know that the company has a problem.</p></div><div class="uce-reply-intro"><h3>What if the buyer says…</h3><p>Explore three possible replies. The completed meeting record below follows the buyer who agrees to a discussion.</p></div><div id="uce-branch-controls" class="uce-branch-controls" role="group" aria-label="Choose a sample buyer reply" hidden>{% for branch in usCampaignExample.branches %}<button type="button" data-uce-branch="{{ branch.id }}" aria-controls="uce-panel-{{ branch.id }}" aria-pressed="false">“{{ branch.label }}”</button>{% endfor %}</div><div class="uce-branch-panels">{% for branch in usCampaignExample.branches %}<article id="uce-panel-{{ branch.id }}" data-uce-panel="{{ branch.id }}" class="uce-branch" aria-labelledby="uce-branch-title-{{ branch.id }}"><div class="uce-dialogue"><h3 id="uce-branch-title-{{ branch.id }}">{{ branch.title }}</h3><div><p class="uce-label">Buyer</p><blockquote>{{ branch.response }}</blockquote></div><div><p class="uce-label">Caller</p><blockquote>{{ branch.reply }}</blockquote></div></div><aside class="uce-branch-decision"><p class="uce-label">The decision</p><h4>{{ branch.handoff.status }}</h4><p>{{ branch.qualification }}</p><div><strong>Next action</strong><p>{{ branch.next }}</p></div></aside></article>{% endfor %}</div><p id="uce-branch-status" class="uce-status" role="status" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true"></p><div class="uce-inline-resource"><p>Have your own opening ready? Check its structure, then use the relevant script examples to refine it.</p><a href="/cold-call-script-checker/" class="uce-link" data-uce-resource>Check Your Cold Call Script <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div></div></section>

  <section id="uce-handoff" class="uce-section uce-paper"><div class="uce-wrap"><div class="uce-section-heading"><div><p class="uce-eyebrow">04 / The qualified meeting</p><h2>Give the salesperson<br>the conversation behind the booking.</h2></div><p>This completed sample follows the third reply. The controller has agreed to examine the workflow. A purchase decision is still ahead.</p></div>{% set meeting = usCampaignExample.branches[2].handoff %}<div class="uce-record"><div class="uce-record-top"><div><span class="uce-label">Illustrative CRM record</span><h3>Invoice approval workflow review</h3></div><span class="uce-record-status">{{ meeting.status }}</span></div><dl><div><dt>Account and buyer</dt><dd>Sample account A: a US industrial distributor with three locations. Contact: the controller.</dd></div><div><dt>Why the buyer agreed</dt><dd>{{ meeting.reason }}</dd></div><div><dt>Current process</dt><dd>{{ meeting.currentState }}</dd></div><div><dt>Role and involvement</dt><dd>{{ meeting.role }}</dd></div><div><dt>Timing and calendar</dt><dd>{{ meeting.timing }}</dd></div><div><dt>Still unconfirmed</dt><dd>{{ meeting.unknowns }}</dd></div><div><dt>Next action and owner</dt><dd>{{ meeting.nextStep }}</dd></div></dl><p class="uce-record-note">Qualification for this meeting means the agreed initial criteria are met. Sales acceptance, an opportunity and a sale are later outcomes.</p></div><div class="uce-inline-resource"><p>Use the same discipline in your CRM: capture what is known, retain unanswered questions and assign the next action.</p><a href="/articles/how-to-hand-off-qualified-appointment-from-sdr-to-sales/" class="uce-link">Read the sales handoff guide <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div></div></section>

  <section class="uce-section"><div class="uce-wrap uce-two-col"><div><p class="uce-eyebrow">05 / Working across the United States</p><h2>Agree the coverage<br>before the calling starts.</h2><p>“US calling” needs a practical scope. Confirm the territories, buyer locations and hours the campaign will cover, then make sure your sales team can take the resulting meetings.</p><p>Confirm where the calling team is based and which US hours it will cover before contracting.</p><p>For international companies testing a new US market, <a href="/services/us-market-entry-sales/">our US Market Entry service</a> addresses the wider expansion question.</p></div><div class="uce-operating-list"><div><span>01</span><div><h3>Buyer time zones</h3><p>Keep the buyer’s location and time zone with the record. Use a dated calendar invitation so both sides see the correct local time.</p></div></div><div><span>02</span><div><h3>Approved contact process</h3><p>Agree the contact sources, dialing approach, recording settings, permissions and opt-out process before launch. Use the <a href="/articles/b2b-cold-calling-compliance-global/">campaign compliance guide</a> to frame the review.</p></div></div><div><span>03</span><div><h3>Clear ownership</h3><p>The calling team handles the agreed outreach and qualification. The seller handles product demonstrations, technical answers, proposals and commercial discussions.</p></div></div></div></div></section>

  <section id="uce-review" class="uce-section uce-dark"><div class="uce-wrap"><div class="uce-section-heading"><div><p class="uce-eyebrow">06 / The first review</p><h2>Use the response<br>to improve the next conversation.</h2></div><p>A campaign review should change a specific part of the work. Here is how we would interpret the three illustrative responses.</p></div><div class="uce-review-grid"><article><span>Existing system, no stated gap</span><h3>Check the problem assumption.</h3><p>Record the buyer’s actual process. If this response repeats across suitable accounts, review the offer and questions before increasing activity.</p></article><article><span>Information requested</span><h3>Make follow-up useful.</h3><p>Send the agreed material and record the permitted next step. Treat it as follow-up until the buyer establishes a reason to meet.</p></article><article><span>Relevant workflow, discussion agreed</span><h3>Prepare the seller.</h3><p>Focus the discussion on the approval steps described. Afterwards, record attendance, sales feedback and any next sales step.</p></article></div><div class="uce-dark-resource"><p>Already running a campaign? Follow your actual bookings through held meetings, sales acceptance and opportunities.</p><a class="uce-button" href="/b2b-meeting-quality-check/" data-uce-resource>Check Your Meeting Results <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div></div></section>

  <section id="uce-takeaway" class="uce-section uce-paper"><div class="uce-wrap uce-two-col"><div><p class="uce-eyebrow">07 / Take something useful</p><h2>Keep the brief.<br>Bring your own market.</h2><p>Use this completed example to discuss the offer, buyers, qualification standard and sales handoff with your team. Replace the sample assumptions before using it for a real campaign.</p><div class="uce-actions"><button id="uce-copy" class="uce-button" type="button" hidden>Copy the Example Brief <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></button><a class="uce-link" href="/assets/downloads/us-b2b-campaign-example.txt" download="callteam-us-b2b-campaign-example.txt" data-uce-download>Download the brief (.txt) <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a></div><p id="uce-copy-status" class="uce-status" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p><details id="uce-brief-details" class="uce-brief-details"><summary>Read the complete example brief</summary><label class="uce-small" for="uce-brief-text">Illustrative campaign brief. Select the text to copy it manually.</label><textarea id="uce-brief-text" readonly rows="14">{{ usCampaignExample.briefText }}</textarea></details></div><aside class="uce-next-campaign"><p class="uce-label">Apply the approach to your market</p><h3>Who would we call<br>for your business?</h3><p>Choose your offer and target buyers in the Campaign Preview. Explore an opening, qualification questions and resources matched to your answers.</p><a class="uce-button" href="/b2b-outbound-campaign-planner/" data-uce-resource>See How We’d Run Your Campaign <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><p class="uce-small">The preview is a starting approach. We discuss the delivery scope with you.</p></aside></div></section>

  <section id="uce-proof" class="uce-section"><div class="uce-wrap uce-two-col"><div><p class="uce-eyebrow">08 / The work behind the example</p><h2>Review the results<br>from real CallTeam campaigns.</h2><p>In a separate SaaS campaign, our published case study reports 11 qualified demos in 90 days, four opportunities and two customers within six months.</p><a href="/case-studies/saas-demo-appointment-setting/" class="uce-link">Read the complete SaaS case study <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a><p>See how <a href="/services/b2b-appointment-setting/">CallTeam’s appointment-setting service</a> connects account research, human calls, follow-up, qualification and the sales handoff.</p></div><aside class="uce-economics"><p class="uce-label">Scope and economics</p><h3>Estimate the return<br>for your business.</h3><p>CallTeam’s managed outbound programs start at <strong>US$4,000 per month</strong> for the published starting scope. Campaign readiness, data requirements and expanded work affect the proposal. <a href="/pricing/">Review pricing and scope.</a></p><p>Use your customer value, margin and sales assumptions to explore the economics before discussing a campaign.</p><a class="uce-button uce-button-outline" href="/b2b-lead-generation-roi-calculator/" data-uce-resource>Calculate Your Campaign ROI <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><p class="uce-small">Already comparing quotes? <a href="/lead-generation-proposal-comparison/" data-uce-resource>Compare your agency proposals.</a></p></aside></div></section>

